  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1027 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the Middle Atlantic states will provide dry and\nmild conditions across the western Great Lakes region with\nsoutherly winds. Temperatures are expected to be a solid 10 to 20\ndegrees above normal for this time of year through Saturday. In \nfact, overnight lows are expected to be around the climatological \nmaximum for this time of year. The main weather concern over the \nnext few days will be winds as daytime surface wind gusts reach 20\nto 30 mph at times.\n\nDry weather is expected through the end of the week as a low\npressure system on Thursday tracks well north of the area. The\nnext substantial chance for precipitation will not be until the \nweekend when a low pressure system developing across the central \nPlains tracks northeast through the upper Mississippi Valley \nFriday night into Saturday. Rain chances with this system are \nmedium to high (60-80%) with the best chances for precipitation\nSaturday into Saturday evening. This system will tap into Gulf\nmoisture, with NBM probabilities of 0.50 inches of precipitation \nat 20-40% west of the Fox Valley and lakeshore.\n\nAs the low tracks northeast towards Quebec it will usher in a \nmuch cooler airmass on Sunday. Even with the cooler air in place \nit will only lower temperatures to around normal for this time of \nyear! The cooler air pulled in on the backside of the low will \nallow snow to mix in late Saturday night into Sunday morning \nacross the far north, with mainly snow expected Sunday afternoon \ninto Sunday evening. A northwesterly wind flow will allow some \ncontribution from Lake Superior. Snowfall amounts will be light as\nthe probability of measurable snow will only be 20-50% and a \nsmall chance (20%) for more than an inch across Vilas County.",
